# Math projection regression-test independent review

Scope: current feat/doc-canonical-math worktree C:/projects/NEPL3-doc-canonical-conformance. Reviewed the final uncommitted tools/src/documentation/markdown.rs diff, original failed regression log, current test implementation and doc/doc-inventory.md lines 36–38. No tracked edits by reviewer. Approved this bounded test-only correction; no remaining finding.

Original inventory identifies four unintended generic HTML fragments: two <T> in foundation and two <Q> in Math. The old real-document test prohibited every raw-html event, so the intentional Ruby and alias tags introduced by the reviewed canonical Math projection made it fail. The retained .tmp/math-projection-regression.log proves this assertion failure. Allowing annotated HTML is required by the projection contract; removing the generic regression guard entirely would be wrong.

The final replacement checks exact complete inline-code values, once each: List<T>, Option<T>, Vector(List<Q>), Matrix(rows,cols,List<Q>), and Option<Span>. The first four preserve the inventory's original regression; the last covers the later span generic. It additionally rejects the exact accidental <T>, <Q>, <Span> fragments anywhere in either unmutated fixture while permitting Ruby/anchors. For each code value it removes only its surrounding backticks in a disposable String, proves the mutation changed the fixture, proves that complete code value disappeared, and requires the corresponding specific generic HTML tag from the real parser. No fixture/source file is mutated by these probes.

Initial review found that checking the five code values alone would allow an extra unquoted generic elsewhere, and that accepting any of the three tags in a mutation could be satisfied by an unrelated fragment. Root fixed both before approval with the baseline exact-fragment rejection and per-value tag mapping. Re-read final diff and independently reran tests after those fixes.

The earlier generic_notation_is_literal_code_and_generated_comments_are_metadata test remains unchanged: it still distinguishes accidental raw tags from corrected code and metadata comments. Production extract, parser options, inventory schema and generated documentation are unchanged by this patch. The replacement is a guard for the identified generic notation problem, not a general arbitrary-HTML sanitizer, and does not claim to validate all future generic spellings.

Independent command: cargo test --locked -p nepl3-tools --lib documentation::markdown::tests. Final result 10 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored, 77 filtered. Git diff --check passed. The initial patch also passed 10 tests, but that earlier pass was not used to dismiss the review finding. Root's clippy, full CI and archive preservation remain separate gates and are not inferred from these scoped tests. No runtime or wider Doc migration acceptance is claimed.
